<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret charts, graphs, and diagrams. Reading: Summarize sections of texts in written form. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret a diagram showing the layout of the town by extracting key information from a text (e.g., "The school is next to the market."). Can summarize written instructions about traditional building techniques (e.g., "First, we collect the palm leaf. Second, we dry the leaves."). Can identify syllable division and apply appropriate stress patterns in two-syllable words, three-syllable words, and compound nouns (e.g., "monument," "streetlamp," "tradition").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows images of a traditional town square and a modern city plaza. Students identify visible structures like a "monument," "fountain," "historic building," "modern building," "street," "park." The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and guides students to use new vocabulary words from Lesson 1, specifically linking them to the traditional vs. modern theme. For example, "Is this building traditional or modern?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a simple map of a town square with labels for a few key locations (e.g., "market," "library," "town hall"). The teacher points to a location and says, "There is a market next to the library. The town hall is across from the market." The teacher models asking, "Where is the library?" and responding, "It is near the park." The teacher also models the pronunciation of words like "street" (/striːt/), emphasizing the /s/ sound, and then "monument" and "tradition" for clear syllable division and stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'next to' the same as 'behind'?" (No, 'next to' is beside).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'mon-u-ment,' how many syllables?" (Three).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a handout with a simplified diagram of a town square (map) and a short descriptive text titled "Our Town Square: A Mix of Old and New." The text describes the layout using "There is/are" and prepositions of place. It also includes short paragraphs describing a traditional building (e.g., the old town hall made of stone and wood) and a modern building (e.g., the new library made of glass and steel), occasionally mentioning traditional building techniques with simple sequencing words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student will be able to interpret a diagram showing the layout of the town by extracting key information from a text. The student will also be able to understand short written descriptions of places and simple instructions about traditional building materials.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text and match numbered locations on the diagram with their names from a word bank (e.g., "1. The Library," "2. The Monument").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read statements about the text and mark them as True or False (e.g., "The market is in front of the school." / "The old roof is made of metal.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students circle words in the text that describe traditional buildings and underline words that describe modern buildings.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information and summarizing, using "There is/are," prepositions of place, imperatives, sequencing words, and target vocabulary (town square, building, roof, tradition, modern, historic, market, school, monument, street, fountain).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students read a short text describing how to make a traditional roof. The text has blanks for sequencing words (First, Second, Then, Next) and key vocabulary related to building materials (e.g., "leaves," "dry," "attach"). Students choose the correct words from a box to complete the sentences. Example: "___, we collect the palm leaves. ___, we dry them under the sun."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Reordering for Summaries):</b> Students receive jumbled sentences that describe either a traditional or modern building's characteristics or a simple building process. For example: "building / a / is / modern / glass / there / of / made." or "the / First, / gather / we / clay." Students read and reorder these sentences to form coherent descriptions or steps, then share with a partner to check.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Decoding Practice (Reading Aloud):</b> The teacher writes a list of target words from the current vocabulary on the board (e.g., "monument," "streetlamp," "tradition," "building," "spacious," "market," "street"). Students practice reading these words aloud, segmenting them into syllables (e.g., mon-u-ment, street-lamp, tra-di-tion), and identifying the stressed syllable. The teacher also focuses on the pronunciation of consonant clusters like /s/ in "street," "statue," "school," ensuring students correctly articulate the initial /s/ sound.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Summarizing. Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a new, slightly more complex diagram of a town square (or a section of it) with a text that provides specific details about different buildings, their locations, and mentions traditional or modern materials/styles. Some descriptions include brief instructions or steps for maintaining a traditional element.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs first read the text individually, underlining key locations and any mention of building styles or materials (traditional/modern).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, one student asks "Where is...?" questions to their partner based on the text, and the partner describes the location using "It is next to/across from/behind..." or "There is a...". (e.g., "Where is the historic monument?" "It is in the center of the plaza, near the fountain.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, students find a section in the text that describes a traditional building material or a simple instruction (e.g., "How to prepare the clay for a traditional wall"). They work together to write a 3-4 sentence summary of this section, ensuring they use sequencing words (First, Second, Then).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs then present their summaries to another pair or the class.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production phase and a quick review of written summaries.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual students receive a short paragraph describing a fictional "Old Market Building" and a small diagram showing its surroundings. They have to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answer two WH-questions about the market's location or features (e.g., "Where is the market?" "What material is its roof made of?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write 2-3 sentences summarizing the paragraph's description of the traditional materials used, making sure to use at least one sequencing word.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying locations from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (traditional, modern, building, roof, market, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the written summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of sequencing words in the summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I interpret a diagram or map by reading descriptions?" (Yes/No/Sometimes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I summarize instructions or descriptions using words like 'First,' 'Second'?" (Yes/No/Sometimes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was challenging about reading the descriptions and finding places on the map today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you learn or practice that help you describe buildings and locations?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully interpreted the diagrams and extracted key information. The phonemic awareness practice for syllable division and stress helped them decode complex words like "monument" and "tradition." Some students still struggle with distinguishing between "behind" and "across from," and need more practice with prepositions of place. The summarization task was a good challenge; next time, provide more sentence starters for sequencing words. I will need to scaffold the summarizing of longer texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading about locations and building materials. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will use the same maps, diagrams, and building descriptions for students to actively give and follow directions and orally describe traditional vs. modern elements in a town square to their peers, building on the comprehension skills developed today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>